window.SHOTLIST={"lessons":[{"w":10,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 10.01: Metric Units of Measurement","file":"math-lesson-4-10-01-metric-units.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-math/math-lesson-4-10-01-metric-units.html","clips":[{"id":"think","label":"Teacher · Think · Amy's counter (about 1 min) · Warm-Up, under the Essential Question","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-think","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome to Metric Market! It's Market Day, and Amy wants to open her juice stand.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Market Day board]\n\nBut the Smudge mixed up the whole town. The Market Day board right under me has five jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three planks]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Mo is building Amy's counter out of three planks, end to end. Each plank is 1 meter 65 centimeters long. How long is the whole counter?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nThat length has two units in it, meters and centimeters. So how do we add or multiply an amount like that? Don't wait for me to show you. Try it your way first, on the Think About It bar. Write down how you did it. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways, and you can see if one matches yours.","want":true},{"id":"learn","label":"Teacher · Learn · two ways on the mat (about 1½ min) · Learn, top (before the Math Crew)","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-learn","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Let's find the length of Amy's counter two ways.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The mat]\n\nThis is the Two-Unit Mat. Meters on the left, centimeters on the right. I build one plank: one meter, six tens, five ones. Then I tap Add plank, twice.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Split the units]\n\nThat's Atlas's way. Three meters, and 195 centimeters. But wait. 195 centimeters is more than a meter.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Regroup]\n\nSo I regroup. Ten 1 centimeter pieces trade for one 10. Then ten 10s trade for one meter. Now I have 4 meters 95 centimeters, and the centimeters are less than 100.\n\n[ON SCREEN: One unit first]\n\nFelix does it another way. He changes the plank to centimeters first: 165. Three times 165 is 495 centimeters. Change back: 4 meters 95 centimeters. Same answer! Pick the way that makes sense to you, then build the counter and add Amy's juice orders on the mat.","want":true},{"id":"do","label":"🖥 Screen · Do · watch me solve (screen recording) (about 1½ min) · Learn, after the Market Measures Board (record your screen)","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-do","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Now watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 1]\n\nTwo times 1 meter 75 centimeters. On the mat I build 1 meter 75 centimeters, then build it again. 2 meters, and 150 centimeters. 150 is more than 100, so I regroup: 1 meter 50 centimeters. That makes 3 meters 50 centimeters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 2]\n\n4 kilograms 300 grams minus 1 kilogram 600 grams. 300 minus 600 won't work. So I regroup a kilogram: 3 kilograms 1,300 grams. 1,300 minus 600 is 700. 3 minus 1 is 2. 2 kilograms 700 grams. Felix checks it in grams: 4,300 minus 1,600 is 2,700. Same.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 3]\n\nLast one, and it's tricky. 8 kilograms 3 grams. How many grams? 8 kilograms is 8,000 grams. Plus 3: 8,003. Not 83, and not 803. The zeros hold the places. Watch for that on Millie's flour!","want":true},{"id":"wrap","label":"Teacher · Wrap · open the stand (about 45 s) · Show What You Know, after the Liter Well sign","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-wrap","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Look how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you worked with amounts that have two units. You can split the units and regroup, or change it all to one unit first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sign]\n\nAnd that Liter Well sign? 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 2 liters is 2,000, not 200. Press Play and fix it so Amy can open. In clean mode, you'll fix it right on the page.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we cross the Foot Bridge, where the trades are different: 12 inches make a foot. See you there, explorers!","want":false}],"cards":{"sci":{"think":[{"label":"Market Day board","tag":"TODAY'S REAL PROBLEM","title":"Help Amy open her juice stand","col":"#C56C3F","rows":[["Job 1","Stamp the measures"],["Job 2","Build Amy's counter"],["Job 3","Add up the juice orders"],["Job 4","Help Millie with her flour"],["Job 5","Fix the Liter Well sign"]]},{"label":"Three planks","tag":"THINK ABOUT IT","title":"3 planks, each 1 m 65 cm","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Laid end to end, how long is the counter?"},{"label":"The big question","tag":"ESSENTIAL QUESTION","title":"How do you work with 1 m 65 cm?","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Two units in one amount. Try it your way first."}],"learn":[{"label":"The mat","tag":"THE TWO-UNIT MAT","title":"Meters | centimeters","col":"#3A6B9A","rows":[["1 m piece","100 cm"],["10 cm piece","ten 1 cm pieces"],["1 cm piece","the smallest"]]},{"label":"Split the units","tag":"ATLAS'S WAY","title":"3 m and 195 cm","col":"#55C8E8","body":"3 × 1 m = 3 m. 3 × 65 cm = 195 cm. Then regroup."},{"label":"Regroup","tag":"TRADE","title":"100 cm = 1 m","col":"#C56C3F","body":"3 m 195 cm = 4 m 95 cm"},{"label":"One unit first","tag":"FELIX'S WAY","title":"All centimeters","col":"#2DB87A","body":"165 cm × 3 = 495 cm = 4 m 95 cm"}],"do":[{"label":"Problem 1","tag":"ON THE MAT","title":"2 × 1 m 75 cm","col":"#3A6B9A","body":"2 m 150 cm, then regroup."},{"label":"Problem 2","tag":"ON THE WHITEBOARD","title":"4 kg 300 g − 1 kg 600 g","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"300 g − 600 g? Regroup 1 kg first."},{"label":"Problem 3","tag":"WATCH THE ZEROS","title":"8 kg 3 g = ___ g","col":"#E04F4F","body":"8,003 g. Not 83 g, not 803 g."}],"wrap":[{"label":"Two ways","tag":"TODAY","title":"Split or one unit","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Both work. Regroup when the small unit gets too big."},{"label":"The sign","tag":"YOU PROVED IT","title":"2 L = 2,000 mL","col":"#C56C3F","body":"1 L = 1,000 mL. Not 100!"},{"label":"Next time","tag":"NEXT LESSON","title":"The Foot Bridge","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Feet and inches: 12 inches make a foot."}]}},"words":[{"w":"millimeter (mm)","d":"smallest common length unit. 10 mm = 1 cm. About the thickness of a dime.","e":"📏"},{"w":"centimeter (cm)","d":"100 cm = 1 m. About the width of a fingernail.","e":"📐"},{"w":"meter (m)","d":"1,000 m = 1 km. About the height of a doorknob from the floor.","e":"📏"},{"w":"conversion","d":"changing one unit into another while keeping the same length, mass or capacity.","e":"🔄"}]}],"poems":[],"teacher":[],"teacherDocs":{"10-01":"# Lesson 10.01 teacher videos\n\n## Think · Amy's counter · g4math-10-01-think\n\nWelcome to Metric Market! It's Market Day, and Amy wants to open her juice stand.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Market Day board]\n\nBut the Smudge mixed up the whole town. The Market Day board right under me has five jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three planks]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Mo is building Amy's counter out of three planks, end to end. Each plank is 1 meter 65 centimeters long. How long is the whole counter?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nThat length has two units in it, meters and centimeters. So how do we add or multiply an amount like that? Don't wait for me to show you. Try it your way first, on the Think About It bar. Write down how you did it. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways, and you can see if one matches yours.\n\n## Learn · two ways on the mat · g4math-10-01-learn\n\nLet's find the length of Amy's counter two ways.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The mat]\n\nThis is the Two-Unit Mat. Meters on the left, centimeters on the right. I build one plank: one meter, six tens, five ones. Then I tap Add plank, twice.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Split the units]\n\nThat's Atlas's way. Three meters, and 195 centimeters. But wait. 195 centimeters is more than a meter.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Regroup]\n\nSo I regroup. Ten 1 centimeter pieces trade for one 10. Then ten 10s trade for one meter. Now I have 4 meters 95 centimeters, and the centimeters are less than 100.\n\n[ON SCREEN: One unit first]\n\nFelix does it another way. He changes the plank to centimeters first: 165. Three times 165 is 495 centimeters. Change back: 4 meters 95 centimeters. Same answer! Pick the way that makes sense to you, then build the counter and add Amy's juice orders on the mat.\n\n## Do · watch me solve (screen recording) · g4math-10-01-do\n\nNow watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 1]\n\nTwo times 1 meter 75 centimeters. On the mat I build 1 meter 75 centimeters, then build it again. 2 meters, and 150 centimeters. 150 is more than 100, so I regroup: 1 meter 50 centimeters. That makes 3 meters 50 centimeters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 2]\n\n4 kilograms 300 grams minus 1 kilogram 600 grams. 300 minus 600 won't work. So I regroup a kilogram: 3 kilograms 1,300 grams. 1,300 minus 600 is 700. 3 minus 1 is 2. 2 kilograms 700 grams. Felix checks it in grams: 4,300 minus 1,600 is 2,700. Same.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Problem 3]\n\nLast one, and it's tricky. 8 kilograms 3 grams. How many grams? 8 kilograms is 8,000 grams. Plus 3: 8,003. Not 83, and not 803. The zeros hold the places. Watch for that on Millie's flour!\n\n## Wrap · open the stand · g4math-10-01-wrap\n\nLook how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you worked with amounts that have two units. You can split the units and regroup, or change it all to one unit first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sign]\n\nAnd that Liter Well sign? 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 2 liters is 2,000, not 200. Press Play and fix it so Amy can open. In clean mode, you'll fix it right on the page.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we cross the Foot Bridge, where the trades are different: 12 inches make a foot. See you there, explorers!\n"},"pics":{},"picCaps":{}};
